#### 2.2.4 Barrel Electro-Magnetic Calorimeter, BEMC

The STAR BEMC [1] is a sub-detector which has the ability to trigger on and measure high \(p_{T}\) processes such as jets, leading hadrons, heavy quarks with its high speed. It provides large acceptance for the particle (such photons, electrons) detection. In this analysis, the BEMC triggered events are used for the high \(p_{T}\) di-electron measurements.

The BEMC is located between TOF and the STAR aluminum coil of the STAR solenoid and covers \(|\eta|~{}\leq~{}1\) and \(2\pi\) in azimuthal. The front face of the calorimeter is at a radius of 220 cm from and parallel to Z axis. There are 120 calorimeter modules with the coverage 6\({}^{\circ}\) each in \(\phi\) direction and 1 in \(\eta\). These modules are mounted 60 in \(\phi\) by 2 in \(\eta\). Each module is roughly 26 cm wide by 293 cm long with an active depth of 23.5 cm plus about 6.6 cm in structural plates (of which \(\sim\)1.9 cm lies in front of the detector).

